{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1451 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fadbb7df4865e7667b7241051161de2ffcd0262229e95da631deed06634b7a85" :edb/db-before-t 1450 :edb/db-after-t 1451 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534763 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:12:23.599Z" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133497 1100 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - t^2$." 13194139534763 false] [17592186133497 1100 "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133497 1145 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133497 1145 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖ = \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{p}(t) = 1-t^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad p(2)= -3,\\qquad p(3)=-8\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖p(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{p(1)p(1) + p(2)p(2) + p(3)p(3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2} + [p(2)]^{2} + [p(3)]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (-8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 9 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{73}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 false] [17592186133498 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1$." 13194139534763 false] [17592186133498 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133498 1145 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133498 1145 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(2t - 1)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(4t^{2} - 4t + 1)dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4t^{3}}{3} - 2t^{2} + t) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 2 + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$" 13194139534763 false] [17592186133499 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t$." 13194139534763 false] [17592186133499 1100 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133499 1145 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133499 1145 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{π}(3t)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}9t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 3π^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\| = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t)$, we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= (f(t))/(‖f(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π\\sqrt{3π}} \\cdot 3t \\\\\n&= \\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{π\\sqrt{π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 false] [17592186133552 1100 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139534763 true] [17592186133552 1100 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." 13194139534763 false] [17592186133552 1145 "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 true] [17592186133552 1145 "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖v‖ = \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖v‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3) + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (1 \\cdot 1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18 + 4 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534763 false] [17592186133690 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308505 13194139534763 true] [17592186133690 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308568 13194139534763 true] [17592186133690 1020 #edb/ref 17592186308575 1319413953476